WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:01.908
Kia ora.

2
00:00:01.908 --> 00:00:04.272
This is the Pharm final, the forecast.

3
00:00:04.272 --> 00:00:07.943
The exam is Thursday the fifth of November: eighty minutes,

4
00:00:07.943 --> 00:00:11.178
and the reading time is inside those eighty minutes.

5
00:00:11.178 --> 00:00:13.231
Forty-one questions, fifty marks.

6
00:00:13.231 --> 00:00:15.533
Thirty multi-choice at one mark each,

7
00:00:15.533 --> 00:00:20.573
then eleven short answers worth twenty marks between them, from one mark to four,

8
00:00:20.573 --> 00:00:22.999
sitting under three clinical scenarios.

9
00:00:23.449 --> 00:00:27.481
Here's what Joan told us, and it shapes every answer in this episode.

10
00:00:27.931 --> 00:00:28.182
One.

11
00:00:28.182 --> 00:00:31.131
A short answer is a definition plus an example.

12
00:00:31.131 --> 00:00:36.465
Her one worked example: what is the meaning of contraindication, and give an example.

13
00:00:36.915 --> 00:00:37.173
Two.

14
00:00:37.173 --> 00:00:38.788
About two lines per mark.

15
00:00:38.788 --> 00:00:43.633
When a student asked about writing a hundred and fifty words, she said, no,

16
00:00:43.633 --> 00:00:45.183
not a hundred and fifty.

17
00:00:45.183 --> 00:00:47.250
That's the I N P rule, not hers.

18
00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:49.188
Simple, concise, to the point.

19
00:00:49.188 --> 00:00:53.646
Identify and describe means name it, then describe it in a few words.

20
00:00:53.646 --> 00:00:55.325
Don't just write the word.

21
00:00:55.775 --> 00:00:56.130
Three.

22
00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:57.964
It's applied and nurse-focused.

23
00:00:57.964 --> 00:01:00.566
What do you assess before you give the drug?

24
00:01:00.566 --> 00:01:01.749
What do you monitor?

25
00:01:01.749 --> 00:01:04.825
What do you say when the patient asks you something?

26
00:01:04.825 --> 00:01:06.954
Her words: it's not a pharmacy exam.

27
00:01:07.404 --> 00:01:07.726
Four.

28
00:01:07.726 --> 00:01:08.371
The drugs.

29
00:01:08.371 --> 00:01:13.655
On her exam-prep slides, under medications with specific questions, she names two:

30
00:01:13.655 --> 00:01:15.395
paracetamol and salbutamol.

31
00:01:15.395 --> 00:01:19.906
In class on the twentieth she added the classes she's taught: aspirin,

32
00:01:19.906 --> 00:01:24.675
warfarin and heparin; G T N; beta blockers; the penicillins; and morphine.

33
00:01:24.675 --> 00:01:28.993
Maybe one question a drug, she said, so don't spend a day on G T N.

34
00:01:29.443 --> 00:01:29.786
Five.

35
00:01:29.786 --> 00:01:33.278
Abbreviations are simple marks, maybe five of them.

36
00:01:33.278 --> 00:01:37.934
And a couple of easy calculations as multi-choice, no working shown.

37
00:01:38.384 --> 00:01:44.054
And the modules on her list: concepts and drug names, calculations, pharmacokinetics,

38
00:01:44.054 --> 00:01:48.657
pharmacodynamics, administration and patient education, the lifespan,

39
00:01:48.657 --> 00:01:52.926
vaccines and allergy, licit and illicit drugs, drug development,

40
00:01:52.926 --> 00:01:56.795
and complementary medicine with the socioeconomic factors.

41
00:01:57.245 --> 00:01:58.555
So here's the order.

42
00:01:58.555 --> 00:02:03.994
First, three forecast scenarios, built the way her formatives are: a named patient,

43
00:02:03.994 --> 00:02:06.221
then four or five short questions.

44
00:02:06.221 --> 00:02:08.646
Then a track for each drug she named.

45
00:02:08.646 --> 00:02:11.136
Then the principles, module by module.

46
00:02:11.136 --> 00:02:14.674
Then her own formative keys, and the rapid-fire track.

47
00:02:15.124 --> 00:02:16.681
The examiner, this voice,

48
00:02:17.131 --> 00:02:19.009
<v Examiner>reads each question, with its marks,

49
00:02:19.459 --> 00:02:21.111
and I answer it in her shape.

50
00:02:21.111 --> 00:02:24.131
Pause after the question if you want to try it first.
